Reading it: r(H1) is the selection's own points, r(H2) points it never saw.
A point-type inversion collapses toward the random control at H2; a
place-organised one survives. That collapse is itself the answer to the
standing critique that OODSelect's subsets are fit to the points they are
scored on.

